+++
title: Nile Delta
description: African river delta bordering the Mediterranean
hook: Ancient writers counted seven branches of the Nile crossing the delta to
  the sea. Two are left, and the coastline they built is now losing up to 90 m
  (100 yd) of land a year.
standfirst: "Egypt's northern fan of water and farmland: two surviving
  branches of a river that ancient writers said had seven, about 70 million
  people, five thousand years of intensive farming, and a coastline the
  Mediterranean is taking back at up to 90 m a year."
key-facts:
  - name: How long
    value: About 160 km (100 mi) from north to south
  - name: How much coast
    value: 240 km (150 mi), from Alexandria in the west to Port Said in the east
  - name: Where it starts
    value: The Delta begins from Cairo
  - name: Who lives there
    value: About 70 million people
  - name: Branches still flowing
    value: Two — the Damietta and the Rosetta — out of the seven ancient writers
      counted
  - name: Country
    value: Egypt, from 1922-02-28
pull-quotes: []
timeline:
  - when: third and fourth centuries BC
    what: The date of bronze coins from the reign of Ptolemy II, found in 2019 at
      the sunken city of Heracleion.
  - when: around 70 CE
    what: The events Josephus describes in his History of the Jewish War, during
      the reign of Vespasian and his son Titus, when Roman troops moved from
      Alexandria to Judea — the earliest, indirect, reference to the Butic
      Canal.
  - when: 2nd century CE
    what: Ptolemy describes the Butic Canal as spanning the entire width of the
      delta, and calls it Boutikos potamos.
  - when: "1799"
    what: The Rosetta Stone is found in the delta, in the port city of Rosetta.
  - when: "1800"
    what: James Rennell publishes his reconstruction of the delta as it was at the
      time of Herodotus.
  - when: 1922-02-28
    what: The date from which Wikidata records the Nile Delta as being in Egypt.
  - when: "1970"
    what: Completion of the Aswan Dam, after which the delta no longer receives
      nutrients and sediments from upstream, and the annual flooding ends.
  - when: October 2010
    what: The delta is photographed at night from the International Space Station.
  - when: January 2019
    what: Archaeologists led by Mostafa Waziri discover tombs from the Second
      Intermediate Period and Naqada II burials at Kom Al-Khelgan.
  - when: July 2019
    what: A small Greek temple, granite columns, treasure-carrying ships and
      bronze coins are found at Heracleion by divers led by Franck Goddio.
  - when: "2021"
    what: Schiestl argues the eastern portion of the Butic Canal was much older,
      and the western portion completed only in Roman times.
