The !!Nile Delta!! (Arabic: دلتا النيل, //Delta an-Nīl// or simply الدلتا, //ad-Delta//) is the [[ delta | river-delta ]] formed in [[ Lower Egypt ]] where the [[ Nile ]] River spreads out and drains into the [[ Mediterranean Sea ]].[[ ^1 ]] It is one of the world's largest deltas.

The Delta begins from [[ Cairo ]].[[ ^3 ]] From there it widens to meet the sea along 240 km (150 mi) of Mediterranean coastline, from [[ Alexandria ]] in the west to [[ Port Said ]] in the east, and the land between is a rich [[ agricultural ]] region.[[ ^2 ]] From north to south the delta is about 160 km (100 mi) long.

== Geography

From north to south the delta is about 160 km (100 mi) long; from west to east it covers some 240 km (150 mi) of coastline. It is sometimes divided into sections, with the Nile splitting into two main [[ distributaries | distributary ]], the [[ Damietta ]] and the [[ Rosetta ]],[[ ^4 ]] which reach the Mediterranean at port cities of the same names. In the past there were several more, lost to [[ flood control | flood-management ]], silting and changing relief. One such defunct distributary is [[ Wadi Tumilat ]].

The [[ Suez Canal ]] lies east of the delta and enters the coastal [[ Lake Manzala ]] in the north-east. To the north-west are three other coastal lakes or lagoons: [[ Lake Burullus ]], [[ Lake Idku ]] and [[ Lake Mariout ]].

Seen from above the delta resembles a triangle or a flower, which is why the Nile is considered an "arcuate" delta, arc-shaped. [[ Aristotle ]] speculated that it had been constructed for agricultural purposes, because the region of Egypt was drying out.[[ ^5 ]]

In the modern day the outer edges are eroding, and some coastal [[ lagoons | lagoon ]] have seen rising salinity as their connection to the Mediterranean Sea increases. Since the 1970 completion of the [[ Aswan Dam ]] the delta no longer receives nutrients and sediments from upstream: the soils of the floodplains have become poorer, and large amounts of fertilizers are now used. [[ Topsoil ]] in the delta can be as much as 21 m (70 ft) in depth.

== History

People have lived in the Nile Delta region for thousands of years, and it has been intensively farmed for at least the past five thousand. The delta was a major part of Lower Egypt, and many archaeological sites are located in and around the region.[[ ^6 ]] Artifacts belonging to ancient sites have been found on the delta's coast, and the [[ Rosetta Stone ]] was found here in 1799, in the port city of Rosetta — an anglicized version of the name [[ Rashid | rosetta ]].

Some of the sites are under water. In July 2019 a small Greek temple, ancient granite columns, treasure-carrying ships and bronze coins from the reign of [[ Ptolemy II ]], dating back to the third and fourth centuries BC, were found at the sunken city of [[ Heracleion ]], colloquially known as Egypt's Atlantis. Egyptian and European divers led by the underwater archaeologist [[ Franck Goddio ]] also uncovered a devastated historic temple, the city's main temple, underwater off Egypt's north coast.[[ ^7 ]][[ ^8 ]][[ ^9 ]][[ ^10 ]]

Six months earlier, in January 2019, archaeologists led by [[ Mostafa Waziri ]] working in the Kom Al-Khelgan area of the Nile Delta discovered tombs from the [[ Second Intermediate Period | second-intermediate-period-of-egypt ]] and burials from the [[ Naqada II ]] era. The burial site contained the remains of animals, [[ amulets | amulet ]] and scarabs carved from [[ faience ]], round and oval pots with handles, flint knives, and broken and burned pottery. All the burials included skulls and skeletons in the bending position, and were not very well-preserved.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^12 ]]

== Ancient branches of the Nile

Records from ancient times, such as by [[ Ptolemy ]], reported that the delta had seven [[ distributaries | distributary ]] or branches. From east to west:[[ ^4 ]]

- the [[ Pelusiac | pelusium ]]
- the [[ Tanitic | tanis ]]
- the [[ Mendesian | mendes ]]
- the Phatnitic or Phatmetic (later the [[ Damietta ]])
- the [[ Sebennytic | sebennytos ]]
- the Bolbitine (later the [[ Rosetta ]])[[ ^13 ]]
- the [[ Canopic | canobus ]] (also called the Herakleotic,[[ ^14 ]] Agathodaemon[[ ^15 ]])

== George of Cyprus list

A later list, from [[ George of Cyprus | george-of-cyprus-geographer ]], names them differently:[[ ^16 ]]

- Alexandrian (Schedia canal)
- Colynthin (Canopic)
- [[ Agnu | agnou ]] (Rosetta)
- Parollos ([[ Burullus | lake-burullus ]])
- Chasmatos ([[ Baltim ]])
- Tamiathe (Damietta)
- Tenese ([[ Tinnis ]])

Modern Egyptologists suggest that in the Pharaonic era there were at a time five main branches:[[ ^17 ]][[ ^18 ]]

- the Pelusiac
- the Sebennytic
- the Canopic
- the Damietta
- the Rosetta

The first three have dried up over the centuries due to [[ flood control ]], [[ silting ]] and changing relief; the last two still exist today. The [[ Delta used to flood annually | flooding-of-the-nile ]], but that ended with the construction of the [[ Aswan Dam ]].

== Butic Canal

The Butic Canal, or Butic River, was an ancient east-west watercourse in the Nile Delta. Its reconstructed length is about 185-200 km, which would make it the longest canal in Egypt.[[ ^19 ]] It did not pass by the town of [[ Buto ]] itself, but went south of it and of the large Butic lake that almost reached the Mediterranean Sea.

The earliest reference to it is indirect. [[ Flavius Josephus ]], in his History of the Jewish War,[[ ^20 ]] describes events around 70 CE, during the reign of [[ Vespasian ]] and his son [[ Titus ]], when the Roman authorities moved troops from [[ Alexandria ]] to [[ Judea ]], partly by ship, in order to fight against the Jewish rebellion.

The 2nd century CE geographer [[ Ptolemy ]] is more direct: he describes the canal as spanning the entire width of the delta from east to west,[[ ^21 ]] and calls it //Boutikos potamos//. It probably reached from [[ Pelusium ]] in the east to Hermopolis Parva ([[ Damanhur ]]) in the west.

The two halves are not equally well known. According to Schiestl (2021), the canal is much better attested in the eastern delta, where some portions of it have been located; in the western delta its traces are more difficult to find. He suggests that the eastern portion was much older, and that the western portion was only completed in Roman times.[[ ^19 ]]

== Population

About 70 million people live in the Delta region. Outside the major cities, population density averages 1,000/km^^2^^ (2,600/sq mi) or more. [[ Cairo ]] is the largest city in the delta; others include [[ Shubra El Kheima ]], [[ Port Said ]], [[ El Mahalla El Kubra ]], [[ Mansura | el-mansoura ]], [[ Tanta ]] and [[ Zagazig ]].[[ ^22 ]]

== Wildlife

During [[ autumn ]], parts of the Nile River are red with [[ lotus flowers | nelumbo-nucifera ]]. Plants grow in abundance on both the Lower Nile in the north and the Upper Nile in the south: the Upper Nile plant is the Egyptian lotus, the Lower Nile plant the Papyrus Sedge (//[[ Cyperus papyrus ]]//), which is not nearly as plentiful as it once was and is becoming quite rare.[[ ^23 ]]

Several hundred thousand water birds spend their winter in the delta, including the world's largest concentrations of [[ little gulls | little-gull ]] and [[ whiskered terns | whiskered-tern ]]. Other birds making their homes here include [[ grey herons | grey-heron ]], [[ Kentish plovers | kentish-plover ]], [[ shovelers | northern-shoveler ]], [[ cormorants | cormorant ]], [[ egrets | egret ]] and [[ ibises | ibis ]].

On the ground there are [[ frogs | frog ]], [[ turtles | turtle ]], [[ tortoises | tortoise ]], [[ mongooses | mongoose ]] and the [[ Nile monitor ]]. Two animals that were widespread in the delta during antiquity, [[ Nile crocodiles | nile-crocodile ]] and [[ hippopotamus ]], are no longer found there. Fish in the delta include the [[ flathead grey mullet ]] and soles.

== Climate

The Delta has a [[ hot desert climate ]] ([[ Köppen | köppen-geiger-climate-classification-system ]]: //BWh//), as does the rest of Egypt. Its northernmost part, like the rest of the [[ northern coast of Egypt ]] — the wettest region in the country — has relatively moderate temperatures, with highs usually not surpassing 31 °C (88 °F) in the summer. Only 100–200 mm (4–8 in) of rain falls on the delta in an average year, most of it in the winter months. The hottest temperatures come in July and August, with a maximum average of 34 °C (93 °F); winter temperatures normally range from 9 °C (48 °F) at night to 19 °C (66 °F) in the daytime. With the cooler temperatures and some rain, the region becomes quite humid during the [[ winter ]] months.[[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]]

== Sea level rise

Egypt's Mediterranean coastline loses land to the sea, in some places up to 90 m (100 yd) a year. The low-lying delta in particular is vulnerable to [[ sea level rise ]] associated with [[ global warming ]],[[ ^26 ]] an effect made worse by the lack of sediments deposited since the construction of the [[ Aswan Dam ]]. If the polar ice caps were to melt, much of the northern delta, including the ancient port city of [[ Alexandria ]], could disappear under the Mediterranean.

The smaller numbers are the ones to look at. A 30 cm (12 in) rise in sea level could affect about 6.6% of the total land cover area of the Nile Delta region. At 1 m (3 ft 3 in), an estimated 887 thousand people could be at risk of flooding and displacement, and about 100 km^^2^^ (40 sq mi) of vegetation, 16 km^^2^^ (10 sq mi) of wetland, 402 km^^2^^ (160 sq mi) of cropland and 47 km^^2^^ (20 sq mi) of urban area could be destroyed,[[ ^27 ]] flooding about 450 km^^2^^ (170 sq mi).[[ ^28 ]]

Some of the delta's [[ agricultural land ]] has already been rendered saline by sea level rise; farming has been abandoned in some places, while in others sand has been brought in to reduce the effect. Besides [[ agriculture ]], the delta's [[ ecosystems | ecosystem ]] and tourist industry could be hurt by global warming, and food shortages resulting from climate change could lead to seven million "climate refugees" by the end of the 21st century. Nevertheless, environmental damage to the delta is not currently one of Egypt's priorities.[[ ^29 ]]

The coastline has also changed its [[ geomorphology ]] through human work: coastal dunes and lagoons have been reclaimed to make new agricultural land and [[ fish farms | fish-farm ]], and coastal urban areas have expanded.[[ ^30 ]]

== Governorates and large cities

The Nile Delta forms part of these [[ governorates | governorates-of-egypt ]]:

- [[ Cairo | cairo-governorate ]]
- [[ Alexandria | alexandria-governorate ]]
- [[ Beheira | beheira-governorate ]]
- [[ Kafr el Sheikh | kafr-el-sheikh-governorate ]]
- [[ Gharbiya | gharbia-governorate ]]
- [[ Minufiya | monufia-governorate ]]
- [[ Qalyubiya | qalyubia-governorate ]]
- [[ Dakahlia | dakahlia-governorate ]]
- [[ Damietta | damietta-governorate ]]
- [[ Sharqiyah | sharqia-governorate ]]
- [[ Port Said | port-said-governorate ]]
- [[ Ismailia | ismailia-governorate ]]
- [[ Suez | suez-governorate ]]

